> {{PURPOSE}}
> Created: {{DATE}} · Project type: {{PROJECT_TYPE}}

Work phases **in order, one per fresh session**. A phase is done only when
every exit test passes and a separate review is clean. Tick boxes as exit
tests pass; don't tick ahead. Rewrite the phase list after the Phase 0
interview if the project's shape demands it — but keep exit tests on every
phase.

## Phase 0 — Setup

- [x] Project bootstrapped (docs, CLAUDE.md, skills, reviewer agent).
- [ ] Blank/template project builds with the command in CLAUDE.md.

Exit test: a fresh agent session can read CLAUDE.md + docs/STATE.md and
state what this project is and what Phase 1 is.

## Phase 1 — Skeleton

- [ ] App/product shell runs: navigation, empty main screens/routes,
      design system basics (type, colour, spacing) in place.
- [ ] Uses realistic placeholder structure — no feature logic yet.

Exit test: the skeleton runs end-to-end (simulator, browser, or CLI) and
every main screen/route opens without error.

## Phase 2 — Core domain

- [ ] Core data model / domain logic implemented.
- [ ] Persistence works across restarts.

Exit test: automated tests cover the domain's provably-right answers
(calculations, parsing, transforms) and pass; data survives a restart.

## Phase 3 — Main features

- [ ] The v1 scope features from PRD.md, built one plan at a time
      (one `docs/plans/PLAN.md` per feature or sub-phase).

Exit test: each PRD v1 feature works end-to-end with real (not mock) data,
verified by using it — not only by reading the code.

## Phase 4 — Data in / integrations

- [ ] Imports, sync, or external-service integrations in PRD scope.
- [ ] Failure states handled: bad input, offline, service errors.

Exit test: a real import/integration run succeeds, and repeating it does
not duplicate data.

## Phase 5 — Polish and ship

- [ ] Accessibility pass (platform-appropriate: VoiceOver/Dynamic Type,
      keyboard/contrast for web).
- [ ] Dark Mode / platform polish, empty states, error states.
- [ ] Privacy/store/deployment requirements for the target platform.

Exit test: shipped in its intended form (TestFlight/release build,
deployment, or published artifact) and used by its intended user.

